I can’t verify that such a paper exists, and I would not want to invent findings.

I’m not aware of a 2024 *Nature Neuroscience* article described as a Harvard–Stanford joint study on the neurobiology of “aesthetic synesthesia,” and the phrase “published … last Tuesday” is also ambiguous/inconsistent with “2024.” “Aesthetic synesthesia” is not a widely established clinical or neuroscientific category in the way that, for example, grapheme–color or sound–color synesthesia is.

If you have the article title, DOI, author list, or a link, I can summarize it.  

More generally, established synesthesia research has often focused on findings such as:

- atypical connectivity between sensory or associative cortical regions;
- differences in white-matter organization in some synesthetes;
- cross-activation or disinhibited feedback models between perceptual systems;
- involvement of attention, memory, and semantic networks, not just primary sensory cortex;
- variability across synesthesia subtypes rather than one single neural mechanism.

But those are general points from the synesthesia literature, not findings from the specific study you named.

